Market Challenges
1. Competition from Alternative Semiconductor Technologies
Silicon carbide MOSFETs and gallium nitride transistors are creating competitive pressure in selected power electronics applications. These wide-bandgap semiconductor technologies can offer advantages in switching speed, operating temperature, and efficiency under certain conditions.

As these alternatives gain adoption, IGBT manufacturers must demonstrate where their technologies provide a better balance of cost, reliability, and performance. Hybrid SiC-IGBT solutions and improvements in conventional IGBT design are among the approaches used to address changing customer requirements.

2. Supply Chain Complexity and Cost Pressures
IGBT manufacturing requires specialised semiconductor fabrication, high-quality silicon wafers, precision packaging, and reliable component sourcing. Disruptions in the supply of materials or manufacturing equipment can affect production schedules and delivery commitments.

Advanced modules may also require expensive packaging materials and assembly processes. These costs can put pressure on margins and make it difficult to compete in price-sensitive applications.

Manufacturers must balance production efficiency with stringent quality requirements, particularly in automotive and industrial applications where component failure can have significant consequences.

3. Thermal Management and Reliability Requirements
IGBTs generate heat during operation, particularly in high-current and high-power applications. Effective thermal management is necessary to maintain performance, prevent component degradation, and support long operating lifetimes.

Cooling systems, thermal interface materials, and specialised module packaging can increase system complexity and cost. Manufacturers must continually improve thermal conductivity and reliability while meeting the requirements of increasingly compact power electronics systems.

